{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPERATOR RULING (2026-08-07): WORKING AS INTENDED — closing as not_a_bug.\n\nThe ticket's premise is rejected. Failing a close on an unmet or unmeetable acceptance criterion is INTENDED behaviour, not a gate defect. If an AC cannot be met, that is a defect in the AC, and the remedy is to REMEDIATE THE AC before closing — not to teach the gate an N/A vocabulary that would let unmeetable criteria pass silently.\n\nAdding an N/A escape would weaken the gate exactly where it is most valuable: an AC nobody can satisfy is a signal the plan was written wrong, and surfacing that at close time is the gate doing its job. An N/A marker would convert that signal into a checkbox.\n\nStanding guidance this establishes: when a close fails on an AC that cannot be satisfied as written, rewrite the AC to state what is actually verifiable, then close normally. --force-close remains the operator's escape hatch, but it is not the routine answer to an unmeetable AC."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786096947572879001,"uuid":"b2e5064a-a343-4a23-a717-cb9f764c25a8"}